Z||000000||DecadenceR||05-25-2001||10:49 AM||||Well, I'm still missing something with the re-wire i did to my fan.<BR>I'm thinking, will the fan shroud of a later model still bolt up to my 84 SE? Maybe it's my fan itself that is causing the switch to melt down.<BR>So i thought maybe if I get a newer setup, i can wore it without problems......||||reg||8
Z||000001||Fierohoho'||05-25-2001||11:00 AM||||decadenceR, I missed your original post but are you running the main juice for the fan through a relay and using the switch to trip the relay? The fan draws quite a bit amps and the relay only uses a 1/4 amp to trip it. If its just goig through the switch that would be why its melting down. Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||05-25-2001||01:05 PM||||Interesting...<BR>No, I had it wired with 10 gauge wire and a 20 amp red lit switch like this...<P>A black ground wire from the - terminal on the fan to the body.<BR>A black ground wire from the ground on the switch to a bolt on the interior near the steering column.<BR>A red wire from the fans original 12v constant to the power on the switch.<BR>A white wire from the accessory on the switch to the + terminal on the fan.<P>Anything you can think of I need to do?||||reg||11
Z||000003||rockcrawl||05-25-2001||01:10 PM||||Yes, you need to use a relay!! Use a GM fan relay, they are proven.||||reg||4
Z||000004||theogre||05-25-2001||01:41 PM||||The fan can draw well over 20 amps when starting. The fact GM put these on a fuse link direct to the battery should tell you something.... Even aftermarket fans from Permacool and others call for 30Amp fuses....<P>You're likely to never find a switch that will take this load. Aftermarket switches sold thru most parts stores and radish rack aren't worth a turd. Also, the current rating printed on most switches is AC. The DC current handling is well below that number.<P>The fan relay has contacts specifically designed for this application. That's why they don't burn up twice a week. To activate the fan, the only thing you need to be doing is switching a greed wire to ground.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-25-2001||01:44 PM||||this is a stupid question / stupid mistake question:<P>that 20 amp switch, was it a lighted switch?<P> are you sure it was a single pole single throw switch? (SPST)<P>  you dont ground a switch that is used on the +12V side of a circuit unless it is lighted. If you put the center tab on the switch to the battery, and one outside tab to the fan, and the other outside tab to ground, and if it was a single pole double throw switch (SPDT) then when you turn the fan off you are shorting the battery to ground - hence the smelted switch<P>  it a possibility - 20 amps at 12 volts is 240 watts - or about a 1/3 HP rating - maybe the fan draws more than that, but you  have to really overload a switch to get it to melt.<P>  if it was not a lighted switch then I think you had it wired wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-25-2001||01:47 PM||||BTW if you wire the fan directly and it runs and sounds normal (whatever that sounds like) then there is a 99.999% chance the fan is ok, then you either have a short in the fan wireing, pinched to the frame somewhere, or it draws more than your 20A switch can handle, or you wired the switch wrong - ie my last post.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DecadenceR||05-25-2001||11:40 PM||||Ken - I guess I worded it wrong, didn't mean to confuse. yes, the switch is a lighted switch.<BR>||||reg||1
